<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Keeping Our Communities Clean – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Keeping Our Communities Clean</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> We Should Clean the Classroom on Friday.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: productive: Create lists of steps for solving problems.<br>interactive: Write questions for a classroom research project.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Can list steps to organize a cleanup (e.g., 'Gather gloves, bags, and bins.').<br>Can make a supplies list for cleanup activities (e.g., paper towels, trash bags).<br>Can write questions for a cleanup research project collaboratively (e.g., 'What day is best for cleaning?').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #e0e0e0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of a messy classroom and a clean classroom. Students are asked to verbally identify items and actions in each picture. The teacher writes key vocabulary words on the board as students say them (e.g., <i>dirty, clean, trash, desks, floor, chairs</i>). Then, the teacher asks, "What day should we clean the classroom?" to introduce the theme.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes on the board, "We should clean the classroom on Friday." and "We will need supplies." The teacher then models writing a simple list of supplies on the board: "1. trash bags. 2. paper towels. 3. sponges." The teacher pronounces the imperative commands clearly, emphasizing word stress on 'bring' and 'clean' as they write them. Students observe how the teacher forms letters and structures the list.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'We should clean' a suggestion or an order?" (Expected answer: A suggestion)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What will we write today? A story or a list?" (Expected answer: A list)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Keeping Our Communities Clean".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short written notice or an email from the principal to students, outlining a plan for a classroom cleanup. The text will use modal verbs (<i>should, can, must</i>), future tense (<i>will clean</i>), and imperatives (<i>Bring, Pick up, Collect</i>). It will also contain key vocabulary: <i>community, classroom, supplies, trash, tasks, group, clean, organize, bags, recycling, responsibility, volunteers</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing plans and tasks: 'We will clean the classroom on Friday.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> The teacher asks students to read the notice silently and then asks, "What is the main idea of this message?" (Expected answer: It's about cleaning the classroom.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read sentences about the notice and mark them True or False (e.g., "The cleanup is on Monday." "Students must bring their own supplies.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match phrases from the notice (e.g., "What day...") with answers (e.g., "...Friday"). This prepares them for writing questions later.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing sentences using modal verbs for suggestions/obligation (<i>should, must</i>), future tense (<i>will</i>), and imperatives. They will use vocabulary such as <i>supplies, trash, tasks, organize, collect, sort, responsible, clean, helpful, quick</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice – Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks for students to complete, focusing on grammar and vocabulary.
                        <br>Example: "We _____ clean the desks." (should/will)
                        <br>"You _____ bring your own water bottle." (must/can)
                        <br>"First, _____ up the trash." (pick/collect)
                        <br>Students write the missing words individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled practice – Guided List Writing:</b> The teacher gives students a list of action verbs and nouns related to cleanup (e.g., <i>clean, sweep, organize, tables, floor, books, trash</i>). Students work in pairs to write short imperative commands or suggestions to create a simple "To-Do List for Classroom Cleanup."
                        <br>Example: "Clean the tables." "We should sweep the floor." "Organize the books."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Sentence Construction:</b> In small groups (3-4 students), the teacher provides various word cards (e.g., "We," "should," "clean," "the," "desks," "on," "Friday," "You," "can," "help," "by," "collecting," "recycling"). Students arrange the cards to form grammatically correct sentences and then write them down in their notebooks. Each group must write at least three different sentences.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Keeping Our Communities Clean".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Written Product (lists of steps, supplies list, research questions).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "Our Classroom Cleanup Plan"</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups (3-4). Each group will collaboratively write a plan for cleaning their classroom on Friday.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 1: Steps for Cleanup (Listing steps for solving problems)</b>
                        <br>Each group must write a numbered list of at least 5 steps for cleaning the classroom. They should use imperatives and modal verbs (<i>should, must, can</i>).
                        <br>Example: "1. Gather all trash in bags." "2. We should wipe the tables." "3. You must put books on the shelves."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 2: Supplies List (Making a supplies list)</b>
                        <br>Each group will then write a list of at least 5 supplies needed for the cleanup, using vocabulary like <i>trash bags, paper towels, brooms, soap, water, gloves</i>.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 3: Research Questions (Writing questions for a project)</b>
                        <br>Finally, each group will write 2-3 questions for a short classroom research project about cleaning habits or preferences. They should use question words (<i>What, How, Where</i>).
                        <br>Example: "What day is best for cleaning?" "How can we keep the classroom clean every day?" "Where should we put the recycling?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups will present their written plans to the class by reading them aloud, allowing for peer comparison of written content.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Pragmatic Focus:</b> Describing plans and tasks ('We will clean the classroom on Friday.'), making suggestions ('We should clean the desks.'), asking for and giving information ('Who will bring the cleaning supplies?').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Sociolinguistic Focus:</b> Participate in group discussions on community actions (collaborating on the written plan). The teacher monitors groups, providing support and correcting written errors as needed.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group's written "Our Classroom Cleanup Plan" (including steps, supplies, and research questions) will be assessed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of written lists and questions (Are they easy to understand?).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of target grammar (modal verbs, imperatives, future tense).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>supplies, trash, bags, organize, clean</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Completion of all three parts of the task (steps, supplies, questions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration during the writing process (observed by the teacher).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Written):</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new word you used in your writing today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing the plan was easy for you?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did writing lists and questions help you understand how to organize a cleanup?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What kind of writing task would you like to do next about keeping our community clean?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The group writing activity worked well to encourage collaborative writing and idea sharing. Students were able to construct clear imperative sentences and questions, but some still struggled with consistent use of 'should' vs 'will' in their suggestions/plans. Spelling of new vocabulary words also needed frequent monitoring. For the next lesson, more explicit modeling of sentence starters for suggestions and plans, and perhaps a quick spelling review game, would be beneficial. Peer editing activities could also be strengthened.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson's written cleanup plans and research questions will serve as the foundation for Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), where students will consolidate their ideas, summarize information, and present their findings and plans in a final project. The lists and questions they wrote today will be used to create a collective classroom cleanup poster or short presentation, integrating various language skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>